' status: IMPLEMENTED in source; native fixtures pass; live sbx/image acceptance remains pending.
' Executor selected Prepared gate for seeding and Retained cache for launch.
' Scope: OpenCode family first; do not change other harnesses implicitly.
' Post-exit sanitization, recovery-checkpoint and durable-publication work remains unchanged for now.
' Existing runtime contract: _spec/defs/opencode/native-v2-integration.puml
' Measured baseline: _spec/packages/lib/opencode-seed-progress.puml
' Credential policy: _spec/_paradigms/opencode-oauth-persistence.puml
'
' BUDGET: proveo-owned instruction seeding and history readiness must share one <=5s startup budget.
' Measure from the first proveo startup step through native handoff, not just creation of a release marker.
' Image download, template conversion and VM boot are separately reported backend phases.
' Moving the same blocking copy behind a spinner or releasing a marker before history is usable fails the goal.
' A prepared compatible store must enter the native client within the budget.
' Missing, incompatible, busy or damaged prepared state must return an actionable result within the budget.
' Bulk import, deep integrity scans and large recovery operations are explicit maintenance, not hidden launch work.
'
' HISTORICAL FAILURE: seed --sanitize and normal launch both invoked implicit store merging before their own snapshots.
' The removed merge helper created full sanitized copies of both stores before comparing their entire SQL dumps.
' It does not compare native semver or cheap schema compatibility first.
' A divergent store returns without recording a completed disposition, so the next invocation repeats the work.
' The migration record's version=2 names the receipt format; it is not an OpenCode major-version guard.
' scrub_store performs snapshot(store, stage) followed by snapshot(stage, store), processing a clean result again.
' Every database_snapshot performs backup, quick_check, foreign_key_check, deletion, rebase, VACUUM and checks.
' snapshot subsequently copies the staged file again for atomic publication.
' Launch adds restore, startup publication and recovery checkpoint before starting the native process.
' It adds three further snapshot calls at exit; those do not belong in time-to-native but add lifecycle I/O.
'
' FULL-WRAPPER REPRODUCTION: 2026-10-09, disposable sanitized copies of the actual two stores.
' Source stores were only backed up read-only; schema/version queries and wrapper writes targeted private copies.
' The native target was a no-op executable that recorded entry time and exited immediately.
' Canonical schema includes session_v2; 287 V2-projection sessions retain 286 imported 1.18.x versions and one 2.0.26.
' Legacy schema lacks session_v2; its session rows carry 1.18.2 through 1.18.21 versions.
' Both stores have credential/migration tables, so testing credential presence alone cannot distinguish compatibility.
' Historical 1.x row versions inside an adopted V2 database must not cause loss of those imported transcripts.
' Private-copy seed --sanitize: 74.819s wall; six snapshots; six VACUUM calls totaling 29.998s.
' Seed digest comparison: two calls totaling 13.143s; rebase: one call taking 5.818s.
' Seed publication: 9853 file-publication calls taking 9.695s; 12 quick_check calls taking 3.887s.
' Private-copy launch: native probe reached after 59.599s; wrapper returned after 81.033s.
' Launch lifecycle: eight snapshots total; five precede native entry and three run after native exit.
' Launch lifecycle: eight VACUUM calls totaling 29.738s; two digests taking 13.061s.
' Launch lifecycle: five rebases taking 6.454s; 16875 publication calls taking 13.038s.
' Both phases logged the same divergent-store condition and continued from the canonical store.
' No sidecar failure occurred on the already-normalized private copies; latency reproduces without that failure.
' snapshot/database_snapshot/VACUUM/publish timings nest; do not add their totals as independent elapsed time.
' This reproduction confirms the pre-native bottleneck independently of OpenCode, providers, model calls or UI work.
'
' SEMVER CONTRACT: read the actual image/native producer version before selecting config or history stores.
' Partition immutable defaults, persistent configuration, history and active caches by harness + compatibility major.
' Implemented paths: opencode/v2/config and opencode/v2/share; other majors and unversioned paths remain separate.
' A V2 launch must not inspect, sanitize, merge or seed a V1 store as routine startup work.
' A V1 launch must never open a V2-mutated database.
' Unknown/unversioned state requires explicit inspection and adoption through maintenance.
' Session row versions are historical content; they do not identify the last database producer.
' The native two-run/resume regression includes an adopted V1 session version (1.18.21).
' Prerelease 0.x builds require an explicit compatibility epoch; do not infer stable V2 from an executable name.
' Same-major compatibility still requires the expected schema/migration fingerprint.
' An unprepared schema transition belongs in maintenance even when the native package's major did not change.
'
' IMPLEMENTED AUTHORITY: a non-dumpable engine-local keeper owns prepared state in memory.
' The host launches it with isolated Python and records its kernel PID/start-time outside writable guest mounts.
' Every managed native checkout verifies that identity through SO_PEERCRED and the kernel process start time.
' Mutable readiness JSON and a replacement same-UID socket cannot authorize a checkout.
' State records include exact producer version, schema digest, engine, file identities and prepared/active/pending state.
' Production keepers load baked helper/version/native paths rather than agent-provided helper overrides.
' Keep receipt format, producer semver and schema compatibility as separate fields.
' The host/runtime owner issues descriptors outside agent-writable shared state.
' An agent-written receipt or mtime comparison cannot authorize an unsanitized fast path.
'
' SELECTED SEED PHASE: verify a trusted prepared-generation descriptor plus native-major/schema compatibility.
' Do not run full backup, VACUUM, SQL dump hashing or implicit legacy import during seeding.
' Missing or dirty preparation requires an explicit maintenance action, not a hidden slow fallback.
' Seed configuration only after selecting the matching-major namespace.
'
' SELECTED LAUNCH PHASE: reuse the credential-free prepared cache on its owning runtime engine.
' Check compatibility, generation state and exclusive lifecycle ownership, then enter the native client.
' Remove the five pre-native full snapshots; retain full private-state and credential isolation guarantees.
' Changing cache retention does not authorize retaining an unsanitized native runtime directory.
'
' REDESIGN: retain one prepared native history cache per major and owning runtime engine.
' Keep SQLite active state on the engine's local filesystem, not a cross-VM shared WAL database.
' Keep the native data prefix stable across runs so ordinary startup does not rewrite stored JSON paths.
' Acquire lifecycle ownership once and reuse compatible prepared state instead of copying all history per run.
' Preserve descendant lifetime ownership and explicit busy-run rejection.
' Keep the existing three post-exit snapshot/publication calls; do not optimize that phase in this redesign.
' Reuse an already-verified post-exit result for prepared-cache retention without adding another bulk snapshot.
' Preserve the previous good generation until the new generation publishes atomically.
' Reconcile host-visible history and engine-local state during maintenance; never merge on implicit startup.
' Treat a lost engine or interrupted unprepared generation as pending recovery without deleting its record.
' Cold-engine preparation cannot silently enter the fast path; provision it before the next launch.
' sbx creates/bootstrap-checks the keeper before managed launch and retains successful OpenCode engines.
' It mounts an explicitly requested preparation source read-only; ordinary V2 launches omit legacy history mounts.
' Changed launch settings retire a prepared or empty idle engine, not an active/unpublished one.
' Fresh empty state can be prepared without importing old/unversioned history; existing old stores require explicit adoption.
' Commands: --proveo-prepare [--source ABSOLUTE_DIR|--empty], --proveo-recover; native flags remain otherwise unchanged.
' An unrecoverable engine asks before sbx rm --force, for every harness. Yes deletes the session.
' The next proveo run creates a new sandbox with the same name. That session does not persist.
' No keeps the sandbox, and the next proveo run reattaches to it.
' A kept sandbox whose image, kit, mounts, clone or env differ asks the same way.
' The steps are carry work home, sbx rm --force, then create that same name.
' A headed yes does not remove an OpenCode engine with active, unpublished, or unattested state.
' A direct checkout keeps host edits. A clone loses uncommitted edits unless they were fetched.
' A screen that fails to open counts as no.
' A V2 share with no database and an older store still on disk asks before an empty prepare.
' The checkpoint step is reported after prepare succeeds.
' The older store stays on disk and is not imported. No leaves the sandbox up and asks again.
' A prepare that already checks out strips --proveo-prepare and starts the agent.
' --proveo-recover always runs recover. A passing check does not skip it or rewrite it as prepare.
' After a confirmed delete, a failed recreate says the previous sandbox was removed.
' Preparation rejects non-V2 schemas before copying, initializes compatible schemas with the pinned native release,
' then runs the existing sanitized publication sequence outside ordinary startup.
' Diagnostics and commit-subject helpers use disposable empty private state rather than cloning all history.
' Docker-only disposable engines do not supply a retained keeper; the managed fast-start backend is sbx.
' Docker compatibility explicitly selects disposable sanitized preparation; it does not claim the sbx cache latency guarantee.
' Explicit import uses a temporary read-only-source engine and prepares the stable engine from the published V2 checkpoint.
' The imported source never remains mounted in the retained agent engine, and its launch receipt stays stable on reattach.
' Stopped or unattested engines are not automatically retired when configuration changes.
' Published-state fingerprints cover the whole artifact/state tree except lifecycle locks and private recovery directories.
' Checkout acquires exclusive lifecycle ownership before comparing that fingerprint.
' Recovery requires a matching producer/engine/database/root and a persisted durable baseline.
' Post-exit writes transaction phase and publication baseline before rotating cache generations.
' Journal updates use fsynced temporary files and atomic replacement without replacing the lifecycle-lock inode.
' Idle prepared caches retain a recovery baseline but restarted authorities never treat disk state as a fast-path receipt.
' Durable publication persists per-file rename intents and content digests before replacement.
' File, directory-creation and deletion intents append to an fsynced linear log rather than rewriting a growing manifest.
' Every intent append syncs the log then its parent directory before authorizing a durable mutation.
' Interrupted publication accepts only unchanged baseline files or files matching its recorded intents.
' Native preferences restore from the retained full sanitized state image after partial cache rotation.
' Lifecycle journals are control-plane files excluded from every history snapshot and artifact link.
' Disposable Docker mode refuses a pending engine-owned cache journal before touching history.
' Launch receipts include the stored sbx agent type; shell/native changes cannot silently reuse the wrong agent.
' Dead implicit-history merge/digest helpers and their unused host forwarding are removed.
' Auth-file sanitization remains an import boundary, not a supported plaintext credential persistence mechanism.
' Recovery reconciles a retained previous-data directory and cleans only private publication transaction directories.
'
' CREDENTIAL GATE: an idle reusable cache and published generation must remain credential-free.
' Sanitize after every native writer and descendant stops, before marking that cache prepared again.
' Raw OAuth/API tokens must not gain persistence through cache retention.
' The host-owned OAuth proposal remains planned and requires its own pinned-connector verification.
' Do not rely on that unfinished proposal to claim a clean or fast active store today.
' Reject a design that only bypasses token removal, drops history or reads mutable data under a forged clean receipt.
'
' VERIFICATION GATES:
' - actual V1/V2 fixtures select disjoint stores; wrong-major paths remain unopened and byte-identical;
' - matching-major adoption verifies schema, every retained session transcript and artifact path;
' - prepared launches call neither legacy migration nor full backup/VACUUM/iterdump/rebase;
' - native session list and resume work on representative 879MB+ history within the <=5s startup budget;
' - cold/mismatch/busy/damaged cases resolve within the budget with maintenance/recovery guidance;
' - tokens disappear from idle cache, generation files and journals after login, refresh and interruption;
' - signal, descendant, cross-engine, disk-full and generation-publication failure fixtures retain recoverable state;
' - missing cache or another engine's record never causes reset, overwrite or cross-major fallback.
' - post-exit sanitization, recovery publication and durable publication preserve their existing behavior;
' - only a verified credential-free post-exit result can become the next prepared cache.
' Performance acceptance must measure successful native readiness, not just a five-second timeout or fake executable.
' Persisted installed-V2 fixture: a prepared 900MiB payload database produced native session-list output in 0.595s.
' The full test took 48.047s including explicit preparation and unchanged post-exit publication.
' After the review repairs, all eight installed-native tests passed; prepared 900MiB readiness measured 0.472s.
' Full Go verification reproduces three Docker run-golden failures and the credential-posture failure on unchanged HEAD.
' Docker plan goldens now assert the explicit disposable history mode and container ownership identifiers.
' Unit fixtures cover owner identity mismatch, forged ready files, cache mutation, wrong-major rejection and private recovery.
' Actual cross-VM locking, retained-sbx startup and rebuilt-image execution require host-side acceptance.
' FK (manual syllables): VERSION 10.8 (29w/51s); ACTIVE 11.0 (31w/54s); MAINTAIN 11.8 (28w/52s).
'
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
title OpenCode prepared history — implemented; live sbx acceptance pending
left to right direction

actor "Executor\nlaunch or explicit maintenance" as OP
component "Native/image version resolver\nbaked stable 2.x + native version check" as VERSION <<app>>
database "Host cache owner record\nkernel PID/start-time outside guest mounts" as CATALOG <<db>>
component "opencode_cache.py\nprepared gate + runtime keeper" as OWNER <<host>>
database "Engine-local active history\nstable prefix; prepared generation" as ACTIVE <<db>>
component "OpenCode native client\nmatching major only" as NATIVE <<app>>
component "Maintenance / post-exit publisher\nexplicit prepare + recover; three snapshots" as MAINTAIN <<app>>
database "Versioned credential-free checkpoints\nprevious good generation retained" as DURABLE <<db>>
database "V1 or unknown history\nuntouched by V2 launch" as OLD <<db>>
component "Preparation required\nbounded, explicit diagnostic" as REQUIRED <<error>>

OP --> VERSION : launch
VERSION --> CATALOG : select matching major + schema
CATALOG --> OWNER : prepared engine/generation
OWNER --> ACTIVE : acquire compatible lifecycle lease
ACTIVE --> NATIVE : reuse; no full-history startup copy
OWNER --> REQUIRED : mismatch / missing / busy / pending recovery
OP --> MAINTAIN : explicitly prepare / import / recover
OLD --> MAINTAIN : only approved import or adoption
NATIVE --> MAINTAIN : after all writers stop
MAINTAIN --> ACTIVE : prepared credential-free cache
MAINTAIN --> DURABLE : one verified atomic generation
MAINTAIN --> CATALOG : issue prepared descriptor

note bottom of VERSION
  Check the native major before opening history
  so startup cannot import incompatible data.
  Keep the schema stamp apart from receipt format
  and old session versions when choosing a store.
end note

note bottom of ACTIVE
  Reuse prepared history on the owning engine
  without copying all retained session payloads.
  Keep authentication tokens out of saved state
  before an idle cache or checkpoint is declared
  ready for reuse.
end note

note bottom of MAINTAIN
  Run bulk preparation outside normal startup
  so the spinner does not conceal the slow work.
  Keep source history and the previous good
  checkpoint until verification allows
  atomic publication.
end note
@enduml
